@startuml
title Current implemented E2EE ciphertext write flow

actor Client
participant "Spring Security" as Security
participant "E2eeMessageController" as Controller
participant "E2eeMessageService" as Service
database "PostgreSQL" as DB
participant "E2eeRealtimeNotifier" as Fanout
participant "STOMP Broker" as Broker

Client -> Security : POST /api/e2ee/messages
Security -> Security : validate JWT + persisted session
Security -> Controller : authenticated user context
Controller -> Service : submitMessage(userId, sessionId, SubmitE2eeMessageDto)
Service -> DB : resolve bound ACTIVE sender device
Service -> DB : decode + bound opaque envelopes (max 64 KiB each, max 10 per submit)
Service -> DB : verify sender, distinct + ACTIVE recipients, single recipient user
Service -> DB : verify accepted friendship, reject self-message
Service -> DB : find/create normalized conversation
Service -> DB : allocate next sequence
Service -> DB : persist message + envelopes + mailbox entries with server timestamp
DB --> Service : committed message
Service --> Controller : response DTO (createdNew)
Controller --> Client : 201 Created (200 on idempotent replay)

note over Service,DB
Request UUID idempotency:
- identical retry -> existing message (200), no second realtime event
- divergent reuse -> 409 Conflict

Conversation creation and first-message persistence
are transactional. Database uniqueness resolves
conversation creation races.
end note

== Realtime delivery (post-commit, best-effort) ==

Service -> Fanout : per-device deliveries (created messages only)
Fanout -> Broker : convertAndSend /topic/devices/{recipientDeviceId}
Broker --> Client : MESSAGE /topic/devices/{recipientDeviceId} (E2eeCiphertextItemDto)
DB --> Client : GET /api/e2ee/mailbox fallback (never acknowledged by delivery)

note over Service,Fanout
Realtime fan-out runs only after the persistence
transaction commits. Replays carry no deliveries.
Broker failure never fails the HTTPS submission.
end note

note over Client,DB
Mailbox, permanent history, and sync cursors are the
missed-event/offline mechanism. Presence, typing,
read receipts, and multi-instance brokers remain deferred.
end note
@enduml
